## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design scalable, multi-tenant data pipelines that handle schema diversity, guarantee data quality, and maintain operational reliability under high throughput.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant schema registry and validation
- Idempotent upserts using Iceberg MERGE operations
- Late data handling with time-partitioned snapshots
- Data quality enforcement via JSON Schema and Great Expectations
- Observability through comprehensive monitoring and automated rollback testing

## Code Example
```javascript
function upsertEvent(table, event) {
  // Validate against tenant-specific schema
  const isValid = validateEvent(event.tenant_id, event);
  if (!isValid) throw new Error('Schema validation failed');

  // Perform idempotent upsert using MERGE
  return table.merge()
    .whenMatched().updateAll()
    .whenNotMatched().insertAll()
    .execute();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, multi-tenant streaming graph pipelines with strict data contracts, lineage, and compliance controls in modern lakehouse setups.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming patterns: watermarking, lateness windows, fault tolerance
- Idempotent upserts in Iceberg with composite keys (tenant/device/neighbor)
- Schema evolution for nested attributes and backward compatibility
- Per-record lineage and auditability; GDPR delete handling via tombstones
- Multi-tenant isolation, access controls, and governance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo upsert for edge state
function upsertEdge(existing, incoming) {
  const key = `${incoming.tenant_id}:${incoming.device_id}:${incoming.neighbor_id}`;
  if (!existing || existing.version < incoming.version) return incoming;
  return existing;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test late-arrival handling across tenants?
- What monitoring would you implement to detect cross-tenant data leakage or drift?
